# ThreadSanitizer suppressions for the weekly `scheduled-sanitizers.yml` run.
#
# Every entry here must name a cycle or race that lives entirely in a dependency's
# own code, with the file and version it was observed in and why it cannot deadlock
# in practice. Nothing in src/ belongs in this file: a finding in QK4's own locking
# is the thing this job exists to catch.
#
# Revisit the whole file at every Qt bump (.github/qt-version) — a fixed upstream
# cycle leaves a suppression that then hides the next one.

# Qt 6.10.3: lock-order inversion between QSslSocketPrivate's TLS-backend mutex
# (qsslsocket.cpp:3100) and the QApplicationStatic guard protecting the TLS plugin's
# QFactoryLoader (qapplicationstatic.h:54/69). Both orders run on the main thread:
#
#   backend -> static   constructing any QSslSocket, via tlsBackendInUse() ->
#                       QTlsBackend::availableBackendNames() -> tryPopulateCollection
#   static  -> backend  in ~QCoreApplication, via QApplicationStatic::reset() ->
#                       ~QFactoryLoader -> ~QTlsBackendOpenSSL -> QObject::destroyed
#                       -> the lambda at qsslsocket.cpp:3115
#
# The second order exists only while QCoreApplication is being destroyed, with no
# other thread left to hold either mutex, so the cycle cannot close. TSan's deadlock
# detector is order-based and reports it anyway. First seen 2026-11-02, in
# TcpClientTests, after tests/test_tcpclient.cpp began constructing a TcpClient
# (and so a QSslSocket) in a process that also destroys its QCoreApplication.
deadlock:QSslSocketPrivate::tlsBackendInUse
deadlock:QTlsBackend::availableBackendNames
deadlock:QTlsBackend::~QTlsBackend
